Z||000007||Mach10||08-13-2001||10:39 PM||||You know, it's the user's end that's bringing things down with Code Red. Code red only hits WinNT, 2k, and XP boxes. Most of @homes stuff is either Cisco or Sparq. That goes for most of the backbones in N.A.<P>You have NO idea how much crap we have to sling because of this BEAUTIFULLY written virus.<P>Signed, Frustrated @home employee||||reg||7
Z||000008||84Bill||08-14-2001||08:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mach10:<BR><B>You know, it's the user's end that's bringing things down with Code Red. Code red only hits WinNT, 2k, and XP boxes. Most of @homes stuff is either Cisco or Sparq. That goes for most of the backbones in N.A.<P>You have NO idea how much crap we have to sling because of this BEAUTIFULLY written virus.<P>Signed, Frustrated @home employee</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hehe Yeah I know, I helped migrate Suburban Cable over to Comcast so I had a chance to see what goes on on the inside. Most attacks hitting me are from end users running web servers and are not savy enough to fix the problem. I was tempted to use code reds back door to apply the patch myself to those machines but I'd just rather poke around a bit  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>||||reg||1
